Save and restore the cursor in modes 1049 and 1048 as DECSC and DECRC do

Mode 1049 saved and restored only the cursor's position, so the attributes
in use when a program left the alternate screen stayed in use on the main
screen, and with BCE erased it in the program's colors: vim leaves with its
color scheme's SGR in effect, and a clear after it painted the main screen
in the scheme's background. xterm saves the cursor as DECSC does before it
switches to the alternate screen and restores it as DECRC does after it
switches back, in the main screen's slot of the saved cursors, and saves and
restores it in the slot of the screen in use for mode 1048. libtsm now does
the same.

tsm-screen.c kept the main screen's erase attributes on entering the
alternate screen and never put them back; leaving it now puts them back.

